theory RHTNAttach
/*
================================================================================
  RHTN session attach -- symbolic authentication analysis (Tamarin)
================================================================================

WHAT THIS FILE IS
  wire Section 9.1 binds a session to a node's keyhash by mutual authentication;
  design Section 18.1's stolen-device analysis turns on the question this
  model asks: can any party that is not S be accepted AS S when a client
  attaches?  The property: a client that completes an attach believing it is
  talking to a particular node identity really is -- no other party can
  produce an accepted attach under that identity's name without its key.

  WHAT THIS MODEL DOES NOT REACH.  It proves ENDPOINT AUTHENTICATION, not
  AUTHORISATION.  There is no patron/sibling role here, no session mode, no
  countersignature and no trust-bearing operation -- so it cannot express
  the failover case design Section 12.6.5.1 actually turns on, where a
  client knowingly attaches to a SIBLING, the sibling authenticates
  correctly AS ITSELF, and trust-bearing operations must still stop because
  a sibling is not the patron.  A sibling proving it really is the sibling
  is expected behaviour; that it may not then act as patron is a separate
  property, and modelling it needs role facts this theory does not have.

  The review plan's Stage 1.1 names the target "Session attach: a sibling
  cannot impersonate the patron for trust-bearing operations".  **THIS
  THEORY DOES NOT CLOSE THAT TARGET** -- see the paragraph above: it has no
  authority relation and no trust-bearing operation, so it cannot say
  whether an authenticated sibling may act as patron.  What it establishes
  is the endpoint-authentication half, which is a precondition of the target
  and not the target.  It is also the simplest of the four, chosen first as
  the model that teaches the tool.

HOW TO READ A TAMARIN THEORY (for a first-time reader)
  Tamarin proves properties of protocols in the SYMBOLIC (Dolev-Yao) model:
  cryptography is perfect (the only way to produce a signature is to hold
  the key), and the NETWORK IS THE ADVERSARY -- it carries, drops, reorders
  and forges any message it can construct from what it has seen.

  The model is a set of MULTISET REWRITE RULES.  Each rule has the shape

        rule Name:
          [ premises ] --[ action facts ]-> [ conclusions ]

  * FACTS are predicates over terms, e.g. Ltk($A, ~k).  A fact with a '!'
    prefix (!Ltk) is PERSISTENT: once produced it is available forever
    (long-term keys).  Facts without '!' are LINEAR: each is consumed by
    the rule that uses it (they model state that is spent).
  * A rule FIRES when its premise facts are all present; firing consumes
    the linear premises and produces the conclusions.
  * The middle [ ... ] holds ACTION FACTS, which are stamped into the
    execution TRACE.  Security properties (lemmas) are written about the
    trace: which actions happened, in what order, with what arguments.
  * SORTS of terms: ~x is a FRESH value (a nonce or secret key, unguessable
    and unique); $x is a PUBLIC value (an agent name -- the adversary knows
    it); 'text' is a public constant; other names are ordinary terms.
  * BUILT-IN message facts: Fr(~x) supplies a fresh value; Out(m) puts m on
    the network (the adversary learns it); In(m) takes m from the network
    (the adversary chose it -- it can be anything the adversary can build).
  * FUNCTIONS come from `builtins` or `functions` declarations.  Here we use
    the `signing` builtin, which provides sign(msg, key), the corresponding
    public key pk(key), and the equation verify(sign(m,k), m, pk(k)) = true.

  A lemma is proved when Tamarin shows it holds on EVERY trace the rules can
  produce; it is DISPROVED with a concrete counterexample trace.  As the
  plan's honesty note says: the verdict is the tool's, not the modeller's.
================================================================================
*/

begin

builtins: signing, hashing
/*
  `signing` gives us:
    sign(m, sk)              -- signature of message m under secret key sk
    pk(sk)                   -- the public key matching sk
    verify(sig, m, pk(sk))   -- equals true() exactly when sig = sign(m, sk)
    true                     -- the constant verify yields on success
  This is the abstraction the plan calls for: one symbolic signature stands
  in for the hybrid Ed25519+ML-DSA pair (design Section 5.1).  The prover
  does not care how many primitives back a signature; it cares only that
  producing one requires the key.  Modelling the pair explicitly would
  double the rules and prove the same theorem.
*/

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  KEY SETUP
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Every node has one long-term signing key.  In RHTN the node's IDENTITY is
  the keyhash committing to its key material (wire Section 2.2); here the
  agent name $A stands for the identity and !Ltk($A, ~sk) binds it to its
  secret key.  !Pk publishes the matching public key, and we also hand the
  public key to the adversary via Out -- a real attacker knows everyone's
  public keys, so withholding them would model a weaker adversary than the
  design faces.
*/

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  IDENTITY IS A KEYHASH OVER KEY MATERIAL -- `wire-format.md` Sections 2.2, 9.1
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  An identity is not a label that happens to have a key; it is the HASH of a
  two-component `KeyMaterial` pair (§2.2: `[classical, post-quantum]`), and
  `infra-client-requirements.md` states the consequence a client must act on:
  "the handshake presents the CLASSICAL COMPONENT while the keyhash COMMITS TO
  THE PAIR."

  So reaching an intended identity takes TWO checks, and both are normative
  wire checks rather than background assumptions:

      h(KeyMaterial)      = the intended keyhash        (Client_Learns_KeyMaterial)
      fst(KeyMaterial)    = the key the transport presented   (Client_Finish)

  An earlier version of this theory had `!Pk($S, pk(~sk))` and looked the key
  up BY IDENTITY, so the symbolic name was born already bound to exactly the
  key that authenticates it.  An implementation that omitted either check had
  no representation: there was no trace in which the wrong key could be
  reached under the right name.  Both checks are now explicit and both are
  mutation targets.
*/

rule Register_Node:
    [ Fr(~skc), Fr(~skp) ]             // classical and post-quantum halves
  --[ Registered($A), OnlyOnce($A)
    , IdentityIs($A, h(<pk(~skc), pk(~skp)>)) ]->
    [ !Ltk($A, ~skc)                   // the classical signing key
    , !Identity($A, h(<pk(~skc), pk(~skp)>))
    , Out(<pk(~skc), pk(~skp)>) ]      // KeyMaterial is public; the hash is
                                       //   what an identity IS

/*  FIRST CONTACT.  `KeyMaterial` arrives over the network -- §2.2:
    "transmitted on first contact and pinned thereafter" -- so it is `In`,
    and the client checks it hashes to the identity it meant to reach before
    pinning it.  Delete that Eq and an adversary supplies its own pair.    */
rule Client_Learns_KeyMaterial:
    [ !Identity($S, kh), In(km) ]
  --[ Eq(h(km), kh)                    // CHECK 1
    , Pinned($C, $S, km) ]->
    [ !PinnedFor($C, $S, km) ]

/*
  KEY COMPROMISE.  The Dolev-Yao adversary controls the network but not, by
  default, anyone's secret key.  To reason about stolen devices (design
  Section 18.1) we give the adversary an EXPLICIT way to steal a
  key, and STAMP that theft in the trace with a Compromised action.  The
  security lemma can then exclude exactly the identities that were stolen,
  which is how we state "authentication holds for parties whose key is
  intact" without pretending theft never happens.
*/

rule Compromise_Key:
    [ !Ltk($A, ~sk) ]
  --[ Compromised($A) ]->              // trace: $A's key is now the adversary's
    [ Out(~sk) ]                       // the adversary learns the secret key

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  THE ATTACH HANDSHAKE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  A minimal challenge-response capturing ONE HALF of wire Section 9.1's
  mutual authentication -- the client authenticating the server.  The other
  half, and why it is not merely this one mirrored, is set out above.
  Capturing its
  essential property: each side proves control of its keyhash's key over a
  fresh challenge, so the session is bound to both identities.

  Real RHTN runs this inside a TLS 1.3 handshake with an ML-KEM key exchange
  (design Section 14); we abstract the confidential channel away and model
  only the AUTHENTICATION, because that is what the sibling-impersonation
  property is about.  A challenge-response over signatures is the honest
  core: the responder proves it holds the key its keyhash commits to.

  Roles: a CLIENT attaches to a SERVER (its serving infra node).  We prove
  the property the plan names -- the client ends up authenticating the
  server it meant to reach -- which is the direction sibling impersonation
  attacks.

  THE REVERSE DIRECTION IS NOT MERELY THE SAME THEOREM MIRRORED, and calling
  it "symmetric and omitted" understated what is missing.  `wire-format.md`
  §9.1 requires mutual transport authentication AND requires the serving node
  to bind the identity NAMED in the unsigned `Attach` to the identity the
  TRANSPORT authenticated -- "otherwise queued data could be requested under
  another keyhash".  That is an application-to-channel binding, not an
  authentication direction: without it a peer authenticated as A submits an
  Attach claiming B and is handed B's mailbox.

  This theory cannot state that.  It has ONE client identity variable, so
  "the identity that authenticated" and "the identity the Attach claims"
  are the same term by construction, and Server_Respond stamps
  ServerAccept($S, $C, nc) on a message it never authenticated $C for.
  Representing it needs two terms and a session/queue state conditional on
  their equality.  Recorded as absent rather than left to read as covered.
*/

// Step 1: the client picks a fresh challenge and sends it, naming the
// server identity $S it intends to reach.
rule Client_Hello:
    [ Fr(~nc) ]                        // fresh client challenge (nonce)
  --[ ClientStart($C, $S, ~nc) ]->     // trace: $C began an attach to $S
    [ Out(<$C, $S, ~nc>)               // send <client, intended server, nonce>
    , St_Client_1($C, $S, ~nc) ]       // client's local state, awaiting reply

// Step 2: the server receives a hello addressed to it, and answers by
// signing the challenge together with both identities.  Signing the
// identities -- not just the nonce -- is what binds the response to THIS
// pairing, so a signature meant for one session cannot be replayed into
// another (the standard defence against a transplant attack).
rule Server_Respond:
    [ In(<$C, $S, nc>)                 // a hello naming $S as the server
    , !Ltk($S, ~sks)                   // $S's own key -- only $S has it
    , Fr(~ns) ]                        // a fresh SERVER challenge: wire Section
                                       //   9.1 makes authentication MUTUAL, so
                                       //   the client must prove its key too
                                       //   and needs something to sign
  --[ ServerAccept($S, $C, nc)         // trace: $S answered $C's attach
    , AttachResponseSigned($S, $C, nc) ]->
    [ Out(<$S, ~ns, sign(<'attach', $C, $S, nc>, ~sks)>)
    , St_Server_1($S, ~ns) ]

// Step 3: the client checks the server's signature against the PUBLIC key
// bound to the identity it intended to reach.  If it verifies, the client
// COMMITS: it believes it is now attached to $S.  The Finish action is the
// one the security lemma reasons about.
rule Client_Finish:
    [ St_Client_1($C, $S, ~nc)         // client was awaiting a reply from $S
    , In(<presented, ns, sig>)         // the key the transport presented, and
                                       //   a purported response under it
    , !PinnedFor($C, $S, km)           // the pair this client pinned for $S
    , !PinnedFor($C, $C, kmSelf)       // and its own pair, to present in turn
    , !Ltk($C, ~skc) ]                 // the client's own key, to answer with
    // The restrictions below fire only when both checks hold:
  --[ Eq(fst(km), presented)           // CHECK 2: the presented classical key
                                       //   is the pinned pair's classical half
    , Eq(verify(sig, <'attach', $C, $S, ~nc>, presented), true)
    , ClientFinish($C, $S, ~nc)        // trace: $C committed to attaching to $S
    , Claims($C, $S, $Claim)           // ... and named $Claim in its Attach
    , ClientAuthSigned($C, $S, ns, $Claim) ]->
    // $Claim IS FREE, and that is the point.  Nothing stops a client putting
    // someone else's keyhash in `Attach` field 1; the wire's defence is the
    // SERVER's check, not the client's restraint.
    // The claim sits INSIDE the signature because it sits inside the
    // authenticated channel: wire Section 9.1 carries `Attach` within the
    // mutually authenticated transport, so it is not a separable token an
    // adversary can re-pair with a different claim.  Modelled with the claim
    // outside, `client_authentication` falsified on exactly that re-pairing.
    [ Out(<$C, $Claim, kmSelf, sign(<'client-auth', $C, $S, ns, $Claim>, ~skc)>) ]

/*
  The Eq(...) action above is turned into a genuine equality CHECK by the
  restriction below.  A RESTRICTION removes from consideration every trace
  in which the named action's arguments are not equal -- so Client_Finish
  can only appear on traces where the signature really did verify.  This is
  the standard Tamarin idiom for "this rule only fires when this equation
  holds".
*/

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  THE BINDING CHECK -- `wire-format.md` Section 9.1
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  "Authentication is mutual.  The serving node authenticates the client the
   same way, and MUST BIND THE IDENTITY FOR WHICH SESSION STATE AND QUEUED
   DATA ARE REQUESTED TO THE IDENTITY THE TRANSPORT AUTHENTICATED, REJECTING
   ANY MISMATCH.  Present encoding: `Attach` field 1 must equal the
   connection-authenticated keyhash ... [otherwise a party] could claim any
   keyhash and receive another node's queued messages."

  TWO IDENTITIES, KEPT APART ON PURPOSE, because keeping them apart IS the
  requirement.  $A is who the transport authenticated -- established by
  verifying a signature over this server's own challenge.  $Claim is what the
  Attach asked for.  A model carrying them as one variable cannot express the
  attack at all, which is why this property was previously unstatable here.
*/

rule Server_Bind:
    [ St_Server_1($S, ~ns)
    , In(<$A, $Claim, kmA, csig>)      // the Attach, with the peer's KeyMaterial
    , !Identity($A, khA) ]             // and the identity it claims to be
  --[ Eq(h(kmA), khA)                  // the SAME two checks, this direction:
                                       //   §9.1 has the serving node
                                       //   "authenticate the client the same way"
    , Eq(verify(csig, <'client-auth', $A, $S, ~ns, $Claim>, fst(kmA)), true)
                                       // ... so the transport authenticated $A
    , Eq($A, $Claim)                   // THE CHECK ITSELF
    , AuthenticatedPeer($S, $A)
    , Bound($S, $A, ~ns, $Claim) ]->
    [ Session($S, $Claim) ]

/*  Queued material and its release.  design 14.1.4 has the serving node hold
    ciphertext for an offline client; what matters here is only who it goes
    to.                                                                     */
rule Enqueue:
    [ Fr(~m) ]
  --[ Queued($R, ~m) ]->
    [ Mail($R, ~m) ]

rule Deliver:
    [ Session($S, $R), Mail($R, m) ]
  --[ Delivered($S, $R, m) ]->
    [ ]


/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  SIGNING WITHOUT TAKING THE PROTOCOL STEP
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The other three theories let a legitimate principal sign what it likes with
  its own uncompromised key; this one did not, and its authentication lemmas
  were correspondingly stronger than the threat model claimed.  They concluded
  that the honest STATE-MACHINE TRANSITION had been taken, when what a
  signature can show is only that THE KEY SIGNED THE TERM.

  With these rules present the lemmas below are restated over
  `AttachResponseSigned` / `ClientAuthSigned` -- emitted by the honest rules
  and by these -- so what they assert is cryptographic authorship, which is
  what the wire actually delivers.
*/

rule Server_Signs_Without_Responding:
    [ !Ltk($S, ~sks), In(<$C, nc>) ]
  --[ AttachResponseSigned($S, $C, nc), SignedWithoutTransition($S) ]->
    [ Out(<$S, nc, sign(<'attach', $C, $S, nc>, ~sks)>) ]

rule Client_Signs_Without_Attaching:
    [ !Ltk($C, ~skc), In(<$S, ns, $Claim, km>) ]
  --[ ClientAuthSigned($C, $S, ns, $Claim), SignedWithoutTransition($C) ]->
    [ Out(<$C, $Claim, km, sign(<'client-auth', $C, $S, ns, $Claim>, ~skc)>) ]

/*
  ONE REGISTRATION PER NAME.  A public label like $A stands for a keyhash,
  and design Section 5.1 makes a keyhash a commitment to the key material --
  change the material and you have a different identity.  Without the
  restriction below, Register could fire twice for one label and give a
  single symbolic name two unrelated keys, so "the party I meant to reach"
  would not pin a key.  (currency.spthy's Subject_Key is deliberately NOT
  restricted this way: a subject holding several keys over time is rotation,
  which is that theory's subject matter.)
*/
restriction OneKeyPerName:
  "All a #i #j. OnlyOnce(a) @ #i & OnlyOnce(a) @ #j ==> #i = #j"

restriction Equality:
  "All x y #i. Eq(x, y) @ #i ==> x = y"

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  LEMMAS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Read the temporal-logic notation:
    All / Ex           universal / existential quantifier
    #i, #j             TRACE TIMEPOINTS (when an action happened)
    Act(...) @ #i      action Act occurred at timepoint #i
    #i < #j            #i is earlier than #j
    ==>                implies
    a = b, #i = #j     term / timepoint equality
    not(...)           negation
  A lemma with no keyword is proved for ALL traces.  `exists-trace` asks
  Tamarin to EXHIBIT one trace satisfying the body -- used for sanity
  checks.
*/

// SANITY (an exists-trace lemma).  Before trusting any security lemma, we
// confirm the honest protocol can actually run to completion -- otherwise
// every "for all traces" lemma is vacuously true and proves nothing.  This
// is the beginner trap the plan-adjacent notes warn about, written first
// on purpose.
// AND THE HONEST ONE.  attach_is_executable above does not exclude
// compromise, so it can be satisfied by a trace in which the server's key was
// stolen -- which would leave server_authentication holding because no
// uncompromised commit ever occurred.  This is the guard that catches an
// unreachable honest path, the defect found in currency and then ceremony.
lemma attach_completes_honestly:
  exists-trace
  "Ex C S nc #s #j #i.
       ClientStart(C, S, nc) @ #s
     & ServerAccept(S, C, nc) @ #j
     & ClientFinish(C, S, nc) @ #i
     & #s < #j & #j < #i
     & not(Ex A #k. Compromised(A) @ #k)
     & not(Ex X #t. SignedWithoutTransition(X) @ #t)"

lemma attach_is_executable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex C S nc #i. ClientFinish(C, S, nc) @ #i"

// THE SECURITY PROPERTY.  Whenever a client commits to an attach with
// server S over challenge nc, then EITHER the server S genuinely accepted
// that same attach (matching client and nonce), OR S's key was compromised.
// The first disjunct is authentication; the second is the honest carve-out
// -- against a stolen key nothing can hold, which is exactly design
// Section 18.1's residual, and NOT a flaw in the handshake.
//
// This directly answers the plan's question: a sibling (any party that is
// not S and does not hold S's key) cannot produce an accepted attach under
// S's identity.  If it could, this lemma would fail with that sibling's
// forged response as the counterexample.
lemma server_authentication:
  "All C S nc #i.
     ClientFinish(C, S, nc) @ #i
     ==> ( (Ex #j. AttachResponseSigned(S, C, nc) @ #j & #j < #i)
         | (Ex #k. Compromised(S) @ #k & #k < #i) )"

/*  THE MAILBOX PROPERTY, and the reason wire Section 9.1 states the
    consequence it does.  Queued material is released only to the party the
    transport actually authenticated -- never to one that merely named a
    keyhash in its Attach.

    MUTATION-TESTED: delete `Eq($A, $Claim)` from Server_Bind and this
    falsifies, with the trace wire Section 9.1 describes -- one node claiming
    another's keyhash and collecting its mail.                             */
lemma queued_data_reaches_only_the_authenticated_peer:
  all-traces
  "All S R m #i.
     Delivered(S, R, m) @ #i
     ==> ( (Ex #a. AuthenticatedPeer(S, R) @ #a & #a < #i)
         | (Ex #k. Compromised(R) @ #k & #k < #i) )"

/*  The other half of "authentication is mutual": a bound session names a
    client that proved control of its key over this server's own challenge.  */
/*  THE CHALLENGE IS IN THE ACTION, not only in the signature term.  An
    earlier version recorded `ClientAuthSigned(C, S, Claim)` and the lemma had
    no `ns` to compare, so an edit removing the challenge from the signed
    payload would not have falsified it: an older signature replayed against a
    new challenge still satisfies a property that never mentions one.  The
    rule was right and the property could not guard the reason it was right. */
lemma client_authentication:
  all-traces
  "All S A ns C #i.
     Bound(S, A, ns, C) @ #i
     ==> ( (Ex #c. ClientAuthSigned(A, S, ns, C) @ #c & #c < #i)
         | (Ex #k. Compromised(A) @ #k & #k < #i) )"

/*  ANTI-VACUITY: an attach reaches a delivery with nobody compromised.      */
lemma a_delivery_is_reachable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex S R m #i.
     Delivered(S, R, m) @ #i & not(Ex A #k. Compromised(A) @ #k)"

// CLIENT-SIDE COMMIT INJECTIVITY.  A client cannot commit twice on one
// challenge.  NAMED FOR WHAT IT PROVES: this follows from St_Client_1 being
// a LINEAR fact that Client_Finish consumes, plus nc being fresh -- NOT from
// the signature binding.  Measured: delete the Eq check from Client_Finish
// and server_authentication falsifies while this still verifies.  It is
// therefore a well-formedness check on the client state machine, not an
// anti-replay result.
//
// THE PROJECT'S REPLAY CONCERN IS SERVER-SIDE and is not modelled: wire
// Section 11 forbids carrying Attach in TLS 1.3 0-RTT because replayed
// early data could re-bind session state.  There is no early-data path or
// server session state here to test that against.
lemma client_commit_is_injective:
  "All C S nc #i1 #i2.
     ( ClientFinish(C, S, nc) @ #i1
     & ClientFinish(C, S, nc) @ #i2 )
     ==> #i1 = #i2"

end
